---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
const title = 'SRT, VTT and ASS: what is the difference?';
const jsonld = [{ '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'Article', headline: title, datePublished: '2026-11-08', author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'SubFixer' } }];
---
<Base title={`${title} | SubFixer`} description="SRT, WebVTT and ASS subtitle formats compared: what each one stores, which players and sites accept it, and what is lost when you convert between them." path="/guides/srt-vtt-ass/" jsonld={jsonld} ogType="article">
  <article class="guide">
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="lede">All three store lines of text with a start and end time. They differ in how much else they can say about each line, and in which players and websites accept them.</p>

    <h2>SRT (SubRip)</h2>
    <p>The simplest and most widely supported. Each line has a number, a start and end time like <code>00:01:02,500 --&gt; 00:01:04,000</code>, and the text. Italic, bold and underline tags work in most players. There is no reliable way to position text or set colors.</p>
    <p>Use it for TVs, media servers like Plex and Jellyfin, VLC, and most video editors. If you are unsure, SRT is the safe choice. If your subtitles are pictures instead of text, like a Blu-ray SUP track, see <a href="/guides/sup-to-srt-guide/">PGS subtitles won't show? Convert them to SRT</a>.</p>

    <h2>VTT (WebVTT)</h2>
    <p>The web version of SRT. The file starts with <code>WEBVTT</code>, times use a dot before the milliseconds, and line numbers are optional. It adds settings for position and alignment, voice tags for speakers, and styling through CSS.</p>
    <p>Use it for HTML5 video, YouTube, Vimeo and most online course platforms. Browsers read VTT and do not read SRT.</p>

    <h2>ASS and SSA (SubStation Alpha)</h2>
    <p>Built for typesetting. Each file has a list of named styles with fonts, colors, outlines and margins, and each line can be placed anywhere on screen, animated or layered. Anime fansubs use it for signs and karaoke. SSA is the older version; ASS added more effects.</p>
    <p>Use it when the styling matters and your player supports it, like VLC, mpv or MPC-HC. Most TVs and websites will not show it.</p>

    <h2>Picking one</h2>
    <p>If you are not sure, pick SRT. TVs, media servers and most editors read it, and it is the easiest to fix by hand in a text editor if something goes wrong. Pick VTT when the subtitles will be played on a web page, since the browser does not read SRT into a video track. Pick ASS only when the styling is the point, for example a fansub with signs and karaoke, and only when you know the player reads it. For Chromecast, the <a href="/guides/chromecast-subtitles-not-showing/">Chromecast not showing subtitles guide</a> covers the WebVTT step.</p>

    <h2>How to tell them apart by eye</h2>
    <p>All three are plain text, so any text editor can open them. The cues look different in each one.</p>
    <ul class="plainlist spaced">
      <li>SRT numbers every cue and puts a comma in the time, like <code>00:01:02,500 --&gt; 00:01:04,000</code>.</li>
      <li>VTT starts with the line <code>WEBVTT</code> and uses a dot in the time, like <code>00:01:02.500</code>.</li>
      <li>ASS has sections named <code>[Script Info]</code> and <code>[V4+ Styles]</code>, and each line of dialogue sits on a line that starts with <code>Dialogue:</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Common problems</h2>
    <ul class="plainlist spaced">
      <li>A VTT that refuses to play in a strict player: the file needs the <code>WEBVTT</code> header on the first line and a dot before the milliseconds. Converting from SRT writes both.</li>
      <li>An ASS file that shows up as lines of raw text: the player does not read ASS, so convert to SRT and play that.</li>
      <li>Italic tags showing up as visible text in a player: that player does not read tags at all. Convert to plain text to get the words only.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If an editor or an upload page rejects an SRT, the <a href="/guides/premiere-pro-srt-import-error/">Premiere Pro import guide</a> and the <a href="/guides/youtube-subtitle-upload-error/">YouTube upload guide</a> cover the errors each one shows.</p>

    <h2>What survives a conversion</h2>
    <ul class="plainlist spaced">
      <li>SRT to VTT and VTT to SRT: text, timing, italics and bold carry over. VTT position settings are dropped going to SRT.</li>
      <li>ASS to SRT or VTT: text, timing, line breaks, italics and bold carry over. Fonts, colors, positions and effects are dropped. A line stacked on several layers, with the same times and text, is kept once.</li>
      <li>ASS to SSA: text, timing, line breaks, italics, bold and underline carry over. The file is written with one default style, and positioned signs lose their placement.</li>
      <li>SSA to ASS: the same story in the other direction, written as v4.00+ with one default style.</li>
      <li>SRT or VTT to ASS: you get a plain ASS file with one default style. Italics and bold become ASS tags. SRT or VTT to SSA works the same way, written as v4.00 for older players.</li>
      <li>ASS to TXT: the words only, one line per subtitle. Styles and override tags are stripped, and each line can carry its start and end time.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Try it in the browser</h2>
    <p>Convert between them with <a href="/srt-to-vtt/">SRT to VTT</a>, <a href="/vtt-to-srt/">VTT to SRT</a>, <a href="/srt-to-ass/">SRT to ASS</a>, <a href="/srt-to-ssa/">SRT to SSA</a>, <a href="/vtt-to-ass/">VTT to ASS</a>, <a href="/ass-to-srt/">ASS to SRT</a>, <a href="/ass-to-vtt/">ASS to VTT</a>, <a href="/ass-to-ssa/">ASS to SSA</a> or <a href="/ssa-to-ass/">SSA to ASS</a>. They all open the same tool.</p>
    <p>For plain text out, use <a href="/srt-to-txt/">SRT to TXT</a>, <a href="/vtt-to-txt/">VTT to TXT</a> or <a href="/ass-to-txt/">ASS to TXT</a>.</p>
    <p>See also: <a href="/guides/subtitles-out-of-sync/">Why subtitles go out of sync, and which fix to use</a>.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
